1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}
B1 is not a forced kill. After 1. W B1 the C1-C2 pair is in atari on B2 but the white stone on B1 is itself on the two liberties A1 and B2. Black does not have to let the C1-C2 pair die. 2. B B2 is legal here: B2 connects A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 into one six-stone group G. After W B1 B B2 G has the three outside liberties A1 A3 A4 and W B1 is now in atari on A1. The supposed self-atari of B2 disappears once the connection is made, so Black restores liberties to the left side and the B1 sacrifice is immediately threatened.

The unique forcing first move is B2. B2 is the common liberty of A2, B3-B4 and C1-C2. After 1. W B2 the C1-C2 chain is left with the single liberty B1, A2 is left with A1 A3 and B3-B4 is left with A3 A4. Black is forced to answer at B1 at once, otherwise C1-C2 is captured next move. 2. B B1 captures the sacrificial W B2 and connects C1-C2-B1, but the connected group now lives only on A1 while A2 and B3-B4 remain cut off from B2. White now has the net A1/A3/A4 against the whole left side and can close it with A1 followed by A3 or A4 in either order. Every Black reply to B2 is met with the same net, so B2 initiates a genuine forced capture of the left side and no other first move does.

