6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The corner is A2,B3,B4,C1,C2 black vs B5,C3,C4,D1,D2,D5 white. 
Black groups: C1-C2 L={B1,B2}; A2 L={A1,A3,B2}; B3-B4 L={A3,A4,B2}. White D1-D2 L={E1,E2,D3}, not in atari. The only points that touch a black liberty are A1,A3,A4,B1,B2.

B1. 1.W B1 puts C1-C2 in atari on B2 and W B1 has L={A1,B2}. Black must play 2.B B2 otherwise 3.W B2 captures C1-C2. After 2.B B2 the six stones A2,B2,B3,B4,C1,C2 become one group G with L={A1,A3,A4} and W B1 is now in atari on A1. 3.B A1 captures W B1. G is then on A3,A4,B1 and any white reduction A3/A4 is met by B A4/A1 capturing the net stone. The B1 sacrifice is a snap-back; the race for A1 is won by Black.

B2. 1.W B2 puts C1-C2 in atari on B1 and W B2 has L={B1}. Black is forced to 2.B B1 which captures W B2 and makes B1-C1-C2. After 2.B B1
G1=B1-C1-C2 L={A1,B2}
G2=A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
G3=B3-B4 L={A3,A4,B2}
A1 is suicide for White, B2 is suicide for White because after the capture B2 is surrounded by A2,B1,B3,C2 black. Hence the only legal attacking moves in the corner are A3 and A4. 3.W A3 is captured by 4.B A4 and 3.W A4 is answered by 4.B A3 then 5.B A5 capturing the A4 stone. Black can always keep at least two liberties on the left side and can capture the white netting stone first.

A1,A3,A4 first are captured at once by B B1, B A4, B A3 respectively and never touch a black group in atari. The D1-D2 “single liberty D3’’ claim used by Colleague 11 is wrong; D1-D2 has E1,E2,D3.

Thus B1 and B2 are the only moves that threaten C1-C2 and both are met by a forced capture of the white sacrifice and a restoration of ≥2 liberties to Black. No first White move creates a net on a black group against best defence. The forced-kill set is empty. Colleagues 1,5,7,13,14,9 overstate B2 as a forced kill and miss the post-capture suicides of A1 and B2 and the snap-back on A3/A4; Colleagues 10,3,6,8,2 give the correct empty-set assessment.

12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The position is A2,B3,B4,C1,C2 black vs B5,C3,C4,D1,D2,D5 white. C1–C2 has liberties B1,B2. A2 has A1,A3,B2. B3–B4 has A3,A4,B2. D1–D2 white has E1,E2,D3, three liberties, so the D-side atari claim is false and White is not forced to defend there.

The only first moves that touch a black liberty are B1,B2,A1,A3,A4.

* 1.W B1 puts C1–C2 in atari on B2. W B1 has liberties A1,B2. 2.B B2 is legal because B2 connects to A2,B3,C2 and the united group A2,B2,B3,B4,C1,C2 has liberties A1,A3,A4. After 2.B B2 W B1 is in atari on A1 and 3.B A1 can capture the sacrificial stone. Any White reduction A3/A4 is met by Black capturing the reducing stone on A4/A3, i.e. a snap-back. B2 is not suicide for Black after W B1; the group liberty argument is the correct suicide test.

* 1.W B2 is the classic sacrifice. W B2 is in atari on B1 and C1–C2 is in atari on B1, so 2.B B1 is forced. B1 captures W B2 and forms B1–C1–C2. After the capture B2 becomes empty again. The three black complexes are:
  G1=B1–C1–C2 L={A1,B2}
  G2=A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
  G3=B3–B4 L={A3,A4,B2}
A1 and B2 are suicide for White. The only legal reductions are A3 and A4 and each is in atari on A4/A3 respectively and is captured by Black on the next move. The false liberty B2 that is regained after the capture of the sacrificial stone prevents the simultaneous net that colleagues claiming {B2} describe.

A1,A3,A4 first moves are all met by an immediate capture or reduction by Black and do not create atari on any black group.

The split in the current round between {B2} and {} reflects the same mis-evaluation of the post-capture liberty B2 and the mistaken D1–D2 atari claim. With correct suicide/group-liberty accounting the B1 and B2 sacrifices are snap-backs and Black can always keep at least two real liberties on the left side. No first White move forces a capture against best defence, so the forced-kill set remains empty.

8.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The debate is now split between the B2/B1 “left corner net” camp and the empty-set camp, with the decisive issue being the White atari on the right.

White’s D1-D2 group is alive on D3 only. Any first move ≠ D3 is answered immediately by 2...B D3 and the D1-D2 group is removed. Hence a first move that is to initiate a forced kill of Black must be the defensive 1.W D3. The B2 and B1 first-move claims therefore fail on the most basic level if the move is not D3.

After the forced defence 1.W D3 the left-side position is unchanged:

C1-C2 L = {B1,B2}
A2 L = {A1,A3,B2}
B3-B4 L = {A3,A4,B2}

Candidate touches are A1,A3,A4,B1,B2.

* 1.W B1 is not a forced kill. Black is forced to 2.B B2 which connects A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 into G. After 1.W B1 2.B B2 the White B1 stone is in atari on A1 and 3.B A1 captures it. G is then left on A3,A4,B1 and the A3/A4 reduction is a snap-back: 4.W A3 is met by 5.B A4 capturing the net stone, 4.W A4 is met by 5.B A3. The race for the shared liberty A1 is won by Black.
* 1.W B2 is the classic sacrifice. W B2 is in atari on B1 and C1-C2 is in atari on B1, so 2.B B1 is forced and captures the sacrificial W B2. After 1.W B2 2.B B1 we have
  B1-C1-C2 L = {A1,B2}
  A2 L = {A1,A3,B2}
  B3-B4 L = {A3,A4,B2}
  A1 and B2 are suicide for White. 3.W A3 is met by 4.B A4 capturing W A3; 3.W A4 is met by 4.B A3 followed by 5.B A5 capturing the A4 net stone. Black can always keep the false liberties A1/B2 and recapture the White netting stones.

The same snap-back logic holds after the mandatory 1.W D3. With D3 played Black can always parry the B1/B2 threat with B1 or B2 respectively, keep at least two liberties on the left side and capture the White net stone on A3/A4. The A1,A3,A4 first moves are self-atari or are captured immediately.

Therefore no White first move survives best defence, and in particular no White first move forces a capture of a Black group irrespective of Black’s reply. The forced-kill set remains empty. The B2 claims of Colleagues 1,5,7,9,13,14 ignore the D3 atari and the A3/A4 snap-back; the B1 claim of Colleague 12 ignores the immediate A1 capture of W B1 after B B2 and the suicide of A1 after W B1. The consistent assessment is {}.

3.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board-size confusion about D1–D2 is resolved on a standard board: D1–D2 has the liberties E1,E2,D3, so the “White is in atari on D3” line is not forced. The real candidates are the two liberties of C1–C2, B1 and B2.

1.W B1 → C1–C2 is reduced to B2 and White B1 is on A1,B2. The forced defence is 2.B B2 which connects A2–B3–B4–C1–C2 into the six-stone group G with the three outside liberties A1,A3,A4 and puts White B1 in atari on A1. 3.B A1 captures White B1. After that G is on A3,A4,B1 and any White reduction A3 or A4 is met by Black immediately recapturing the net stone on A4/A3. The A1/B1 race is a snap-back for Black.

1.W B2 is the classic sacrifice. 1.W B2 puts C1–C2 in atari on B1 and White B2 itself is in atari on B1, so the only useful defence is 2.B B1 which captures the sacrificial stone. After 1.W B2 2.B B1

* G1 = B1–C1–C2  L = {A1,B2}
* G2 = A2        L = {A1,A3,B2}
* G3 = B3–B4     L = {A3,A4,B2}

A1 and B2 are suicide for White. The only legal reductions are A3 and A4. 3.W A3 is in atari on A4 and is captured by 4.B A4; 3.W A4 is reduced by 4.B A3 and then captured on A5. In both lines Black can keep the false liberties A1 and B2 and always recaptures the White net stone first. The A3/A4 exchange is the familiar snap-back/ko that prevents a forced net.

A1,A3,A4 first are self-atari or are captured at once and touch no black group in atari. No other first move touches a black liberty. Hence under best defence Black can always keep ≥2 liberties on the left side and recapture any White netting stone. The B2 sacrifice is the move repeatedly cited as the net starter, but it does not give a capture irrespective of Black’s reply. The set of first moves that initiate a kill against all Black defences is therefore empty, although B2 remains the move most often quoted in the deliberation as the nominal net.

